\title{Exact Solution-Space Correspondences for Logic-Encoded Jigsaw Puzzles}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study an abstract jigsaw construction associated with finite Boolean formulas. Variables are represented by complementary assignment choices, clauses by components that fit exactly when at least one incident literal is true, and two additional pieces enforce the designated boundary. The principal result is a canonical bijection between satisfying assignments on a fixed finite variable set and valid assembly recipes. Thus the construction preserves the exact number of witnesses and preserves uniqueness. A second result identifies a global involution: simultaneously negating every assigned Boolean value and every literal polarity preserves satisfaction; on puzzle interfaces this corresponds to exchanging tabs and blanks. For the formula $(x_0\lor x_1\lor\neg x_2)\land(\neg x_0\lor x_2)$, the construction has ten pieces and five assembly recipes. We carefully distinguish these established results from the broader conjecture that unrestricted geometric rectangular jigsaw assembly is NP-complete. The latter still requires polynomial-size planar gadgets, rigid framing, and control of geometric automorphisms.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Jigsaw assembly and Boolean satisfiability have the same broad logical shape: a candidate is assembled from local choices, while validity requires all local constraints to hold simultaneously. In a jigsaw, adjacent edges must complement one another and the boundary must close. In a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form, at least one literal must satisfy each clause.

There are several possible strengths of correspondence. Equisatisfiability preserves only existence. Witness preservation maps individual solutions. A parsimonious correspondence gives a bijection of complete solution spaces and therefore preserves counts and uniqueness. The abstract formula-indexed assembly model considered here attains this strongest level.

The model isolates logical compatibility from unrestricted Euclidean placement. Each variable contributes two assignment pieces, each clause contributes one clause piece, and two additional pieces designate opposite corners. The decisive local property is that a clause piece fits a selected assignment exactly when the clause contains a true literal. Once this property is imposed, a satisfying assignment itself is an assembly recipe, and conversely.

We also analyze complementation. Negating both every assignment value and every literal polarity preserves each literal's truth. Preservation then propagates through clauses and formulas and, through the solution-space correspondence, becomes a global tab--blank symmetry.

These statements establish the logical core of a parsimonious reduction, not NP-completeness for unrestricted geometric jigsaws. A geometric theorem additionally requires finite encodings, polynomial construction, planar wires and clauses, management of crossover and fan-out, and a rigid frame that removes unintended placements and symmetries.

\section{Boolean and assembly preliminaries}
\subsection{Boolean syntax and semantics}
Let $\B=\{0,1\}$, where $0$ is false and $1$ is true.

\begin{definition}
An \emph{assignment} is a function $a:\mathbb N\to\B$. A \emph{literal} is a pair $\ell=(i,p)$, with $i\in\mathbb N$ and $p\in\B$. The pair $(i,1)$ denotes $x_i$, and $(i,0)$ denotes $\neg x_i$. The literal is satisfied by $a$ exactly when $a(i)=p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{clause} is a finite list of literals and is satisfied when at least one listed literal is satisfied:
\[
\SatClause(a,C)\iff \exists(i,p)\in C\text{ such that }a(i)=p.
\]
A \emph{formula} is a finite list of clauses and is satisfied when every clause is satisfied:
\[
\Sat(a,F)\iff \forall C\in F,\ \SatClause(a,C).
\]
\end{definition}

Fix $n\in\mathbb N$. A finite assignment is a function $b:\{0,\ldots,n-1\}\to\B$. Its canonical extension $\widetilde b:\mathbb N\to\B$ is
\[
\widetilde b(i)=\begin{cases}b(i),&i<n,\\0,&i\ge n.\end{cases}
\]
For a well-scoped formula all indices are below $n$, but this convention makes every finite solution space unambiguous.

\subsection{The abstract jigsaw construction}
A jigsaw edge may be flat, a tab, or a complementary blank. For a formula with $n$ variables and $m$ clauses, the inventory has two assignment pieces per variable, one piece per clause, and two designated boundary pieces. Hence
\[
N=2n+m+2.
\]
The construction is specified by its essential local property.

\begin{proposition}[Clause-Fit Property]
For every assignment $a$ and clause $C$, the clause component associated with $C$ fits the interfaces selected by $a$ if and only if $C$ is satisfied by $a$:
\[
\Fits(a,C)\iff \exists(i,p)\in C\text{ such that }a(i)=p.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{valid assembly recipe} for $F$ is a finite assignment $b$ such that every clause piece fits under $\widetilde b$:
\[
\Assembled(b,F)\iff \forall C\in F,\ \Fits(\widetilde b,C).
\]
The associated abstract puzzle is \emph{solvable} if at least one valid assembly recipe exists.
\end{definition}

This is an assignment-level notion of assembly; it does not silently assume that unrestricted planar geometry realizes the predicate.

Define the finite spaces
\[
\mathcal S_n(F)=\{b:\{0,\ldots,n-1\}\to\B\mid \Sat(\widetilde b,F)\}
\]
and
\[
\mathcal A_n(F)=\{b:\{0,\ldots,n-1\}\to\B\mid \Assembled(b,F)\}.
\]
Both are subsets of the $2^n$ Boolean assignments.

\section{Exact solution-space preservation}
\begin{theorem}[Assembly--Assignment Correspondence]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$ and every finite Boolean formula $F$, there is a canonical bijection
\[
\Phi_{n,F}:\mathcal A_n(F)\longrightarrow\mathcal S_n(F).
\]
It preserves the underlying assignment: $\Phi_{n,F}(b)=b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $b\in\mathcal A_n(F)$, every clause piece fits under $\widetilde b$. The Clause-Fit Property says that each clause contains a literal satisfied by $\widetilde b$, so $b\in\mathcal S_n(F)$. Conversely, if $b\in\mathcal S_n(F)$, each clause contains a satisfied literal, and the reverse implication of the Clause-Fit Property shows that every clause piece fits. Both maps retain $b$ unchanged, so their composites are identities.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact Solution Count]
For every $n$ and $F$,
\[
|\mathcal A_n(F)|=|\mathcal S_n(F)|.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two finite sets are bijective by the preceding theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness Preservation]
The assembly-recipe space $\mathcal A_n(F)$ has exactly one element if and only if the satisfying-assignment space $\mathcal S_n(F)$ has exactly one element.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A bijection transports existence and equality in both directions, and therefore transports the singleton property.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Existence Preservation]
The abstract puzzle associated with $F$ is solvable if and only if $F$ has a satisfying assignment on its declared variables.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Bijections preserve nonemptiness.
\end{proof}

Thus existence is only the coarsest shadow of the full correspondence. The translation neither loses witnesses nor introduces extra ones.

\section{Global complementation}
For an assignment $a$, define $\bar a(i)=1-a(i)$. For a literal $\ell=(i,p)$, define $\bar\ell=(i,1-p)$. Complement a clause or formula literalwise. Each operation is involutive.

\begin{lemma}[Literal Complementation]
For every assignment $a$ and literal $\ell$,
\[
\Sat(\bar a,\bar\ell)\iff\Sat(a,\ell),
\]
where $\Sat$ at literal level denotes literal satisfaction.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Writing $\ell=(i,p)$, the left side is $1-a(i)=1-p$, which is equivalent to $a(i)=p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Clause Complementation]
For every assignment $a$ and clause $C$,
\[
\SatClause(\bar a,\bar C)\iff\SatClause(a,C).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Complementation pairs the literals of $C$ with those of $\bar C$, and Literal Complementation preserves the truth of every pair. An existential witness therefore transports in both directions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Formula Complementation]
For every assignment $a$ and formula $F$,
\[
\Sat(\bar a,\bar F)\iff\Sat(a,F).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pair every clause $C$ in $F$ with $\bar C$ in $\bar F$ and apply Clause Complementation. Universal quantification over clauses preserves the equivalence.
\end{proof}

Because complementation is its own inverse, this theorem gives a bijection between the satisfying assignments of $F$ and $\bar F$.

\begin{theorem}[Tab--Blank Solvability Symmetry]
The abstract puzzle associated with $\bar F$ is solvable if and only if the abstract puzzle associated with $F$ is solvable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A solution of the puzzle for $F$ corresponds to an assignment $a$ satisfying $F$. Formula Complementation gives a satisfying assignment $\bar a$ for $\bar F$, which corresponds to a solution of the complemented puzzle. The converse follows by applying the same involution again.
\end{proof}

This is transport between two solution spaces. It does not assert a free action on one space: self-dual formulas or frames may create exceptional behavior.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
To evaluate a candidate assignment, compare each variable value with each literal polarity, accept a clause when any comparison succeeds, and accept the formula when all clauses succeed. By the Clause-Fit Property, this also tests whether the assignment is an assembly recipe. If
\[
L=\sum_{C\in F}|C|
\]
is the number of literal occurrences, one test takes $O(L)$ time and constant auxiliary space apart from the input.

Exhaustive enumeration visits all $2^n$ assignments, tests each, and retains or counts the successful ones. Its running time is $O(2^nL)$. Storing $K$ solutions requires $O(nK)$ bits, while counting alone uses $O(n)$ working space.

Complement transport flips $n$ assignment bits and $L$ literal polarities, taking $O(n+L)$ time. Repeating it restores the input exactly.

\section{A ten-piece example}
Let
\[
F=(x_0\lor x_1\lor\neg x_2)\land(\neg x_0\lor x_2).
\]
Here $n=3$ and $m=2$, so the inventory has
\[
2n+m+2=2(3)+2+2=10
\]
pieces. The assignment $a=(0,1,0)$ satisfies the first clause through $x_1$ and the second through $\neg x_0$. It therefore determines a valid assembly recipe.

Exhaustive enumeration gives exactly five solutions:
\[
(0,0,0),\quad(0,1,0),\quad(0,1,1),\quad(1,0,1),\quad(1,1,1).
\]
The exact counting theorem consequently gives five assembly recipes.

The complemented formula is
\[
\bar F=(\neg x_0\lor\neg x_1\lor x_2)\land(x_0\lor\neg x_2).
\]
The witness $(0,1,0)$ is transported to $(1,0,1)$, which satisfies $\bar F$ through $\neg x_1$ and $x_0$. Complementation similarly pairs all five solutions.

\section{Complexity interpretation and limitations}
The abstract correspondence has the witness behavior desired of a parsimonious reduction. It is not, by itself, a geometric NP-completeness theorem. Such a theorem must specify a finite encoding, prove polynomial-time certificate verification, and translate each formula to polynomially many physical pieces.

The missing geometric tasks include:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item wire gadgets that transmit one Boolean state without admitting a third;
\item fan-out gadgets that copy repeated variables consistently;
\item crossover gadgets or a planar incidence construction;
\item clause gadgets accepting exactly patterns with at least one true input;
\item rigid framing that prevents unintended translations, rotations, reflections, and boundary placements; and
\item control of internal gadget configurations and interchangeable copies so witness counts are not multiplied unpredictably.
\end{enumerate}

The bijection localizes these burdens. If every geometric clause gadget uniquely realizes the Clause-Fit Property and composition creates no automorphisms, the assignment-level theorem supplies exact witness preservation. If each logical witness instead generates a fixed number $q$ of geometric assemblies, counting is preserved up to that computable factor.

Accordingly, the result is an exact theorem about a formula-indexed abstract puzzle model and a foundation for, rather than completion of, the geometric hardness conjecture.

\section{Applications and broader structures}
\subsection{Counting and uniqueness}
Any method counting abstract assembly recipes also counts satisfying assignments for the encoded formula. Distinctions among zero, one, and many assignments transfer exactly. A geometric implementation would retain this consequence only after its symmetries were eliminated or explicitly factored out.

\subsection{Complementary orbits}
For a framed puzzle not isomorphic to its global tab--blank complement, one expects complementation to act freely on the disjoint union of the two assembly spaces, forcing their combined cardinality to be even. The proven symmetry supplies the transport map, but classifying self-dual exceptions remains necessary.

\subsection{Boundary conservation and surfaces}
Give complementary oriented edges opposite signed potentials. Interior matches cancel pairwise. On a rectangle this constrains the exposed boundary. On an orientable surface, residual flux around noncontractible cycles may define a cohomology class. A torus should have two independent flux coordinates. This proposed extension interprets local edge cancellation as a global topological obstruction.

\subsection{Solution complexes}
Enrich the solution set by making satisfying assignments vertices and joining assignments that differ in one variable. Fill a $k$-cube when $k$ independent flips remain satisfying. The resulting cubical complex records connectivity and higher-dimensional families of choices. A rigid parsimonious geometric construction may preserve this complex up to deformation retraction, strengthening cardinality preservation to topological preservation.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The parsimony argument is local: apply the Clause-Fit Property independently to every clause. A geometric implementation therefore need not reproduce the logical proof; it must realize each local equivalence and prove that composition introduces no unintended freedom.

The complement theorem is local as well. The Boolean identity
\[
1-a(i)=1-p\iff a(i)=p
\]
lifts through existential quantification to clauses, through universal quantification to formulas, and through the assembly correspondence to puzzles. Tab--blank exchange is thus the physical manifestation of Boolean negation, not merely a visual analogy.

The immediate research goal is a polynomial-size realization by non-rotatable square pieces, finitely many colored edge types, and a fixed outer frame. It should be tested for parsimony rather than mere equisatisfiability. Further goals include classifying self-dual puzzles under complementation, constructing homology-valued edge invariants on surfaces, and preserving the cubical topology generated by single-variable flips.

\section{Conclusion}
For this abstract logic-encoded construction, assembly recipes and satisfying assignments are identical finite witnesses in two vocabularies. Their canonical bijection preserves existence, exact count, and uniqueness. Simultaneous complementation of assignments and literal polarities preserves satisfaction and induces a global tab--blank symmetry. The three-variable example realizes these facts with ten pieces and five solutions.

The geometric NP-completeness program remains open at the gadget level. The established results identify its exact logical foundation and separate it cleanly from the outstanding questions of planar communication, rigid boundaries, controlled symmetries, and topological structure.

\end{document}

